<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-normal> {
  ex:l5-n09a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n09a-heading, ex:l5-n09a-plot, ex:l5-n09a-math ; cd:narration "To estimate concentration from analysed samples, we need to understand the analytical contribution to variation. For one homogeneous sample, write the measurement result as fixed true concentration plus constant bias plus random error. Normal zero-mean error is an assumption, not a natural law. The teaching curve specifies analytical mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L; it is not a histogram, simulation or fitted measurement result. We are not describing concentrations at different environmental sites or the sampling distribution of the water-body mean estimator. A bell shape does not demonstrate zero bias. Independent errors are an additional assumption for a joint model of repeats. Drift, matrix effects, outliers, mixtures and correlation can invalidate this model. Its real-line support permits mathematically negative measurement results; reconsider adequacy near physical or detection limits. The curve's tails continue beyond the drawing. Next separate analytical centre from spread, because a precise laboratory result can still be biased and its sample can still be unrepresentative."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n09b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n09b-heading, ex:l5-n09b-plot, ex:l5-n09b-math ; cd:narration "The preceding model needed a mean and an SD. Keep the nitrate measurement target and compare what changing each parameter does. On shared axes, the narrow peak at 10.0 mg/L is the baseline with SD 0.30 mg/L. Moving the mean to 10.6 mg/L shifts the curve without changing SD. Keeping mean 10.0 but doubling SD to 0.60 mg/L broadens the curve and halves its peak. These are alternative specified models, not new samples or parameter estimates. Every complete density has area one, so a lower peak does not mean less total probability. Normal notation uses variance as its second parameter; SD has concentration units and variance has squared units. True concentration and bias enter only through their sum and cannot be separated from repeat results alone. The finite drawing does not truncate the mathematical support. We now return to the baseline curve and write the density formula that lets us calculate its probabilities."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n09c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n09c-heading, ex:l5-n09c-plot, ex:l5-n09c-math ; cd:narration "The same analytical model can now be written as a density. Keep mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L. Subtracting the mean measures displacement from the centre; dividing its square by variance makes the exponent dimensionless. The minus sign makes density decrease away from the mean, and the prefactor normalizes total area to one. SD is positive and the maximum about 1.3298 L/mg is a density height, not a point probability greater than one. Areas describe probabilities; the drawing coordinates evaluate the formula and are not observations. Normality remains an assumption and the tails extend beyond the plot. We next practise probability and quantile calculations under this specified model. They are tools for understanding reference distributions, not estimates of the unknown water-body mean. We then return to the environmental sampling question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n10a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n10a-heading, ex:l5-n10a-plot, ex:l5-n10a-math ; cd:narration "This supporting calculation starts with specified analytical parameters, not with an estimated environmental mean. With mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L, the illustrative threshold 10.45 mg/L is 1.5 SDs above the mean. It is not a legal or safety limit. Standardization gives standard normal mean zero and variance one and preserves the event probability. The standard-normal CDF at 1.5 is about 0.9331928, leaving an upper tail of about 0.0668072 or 6.68 percent. The calculation uses the full normal CDF, not the polygon shaded only up to 11.2 mg/L; the omitted tail is about 0.003167 percent. Including an endpoint makes no difference in a continuous model. This is not a confidence interval for the unknown mean, nor a probability that the water body's mean exceeds 10.45 mg/L. A model probability for individual results must not be read backwards as a probability for an unknown parameter. Next reverse the probability-to-threshold calculation, then use the distinction to return to estimation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
